<!--
  FS.GG.Kit — the coordination kit as ONE versioned package (ADR-0062).

  WHAT THIS REPLACES. Today the coordination kit (the shared skills + the fsgg-coord client + the
  engine tool manifest) is delivered to N receivers by byte-identical file copy: scripts/coordination-sync
  writes it into each `receives: coordination-kit` repo, the coordination-coherence gate fails a
  receiver whose copy drifted, and coordination-propagate opens a rolling sync PR in every receiver on
  each hub change. The report (docs/reports/2026-07-20-*) measured 80-116 sync commits per repo per ten
  days: one hub commit multiplied into N downstream PRs. ADR-0062 Option A ships the kit as a versioned
  artifact instead — receivers REFERENCE it and pick up a change through the Renovate + dispatch fabric
  every other package already uses, exactly as the fsgg-coord CLI already did (ADR-0034 §4.4).

  DERIVED, NOT RESTATED (ADR-0058). This project packs no committed list of kit files. `stage-kit.sh`
  reads the `kit:` rows of registry/repos.yml — the ONE manifest coordination-sync also reads, via the
  same scripts/repos.sh reader — and stages exactly that content-addressed set at pack time. The two
  distribution paths cannot pack different kits while both exist, and a kit row added or retired needs
  no edit here.

  THIS IS PR #1 OF THE MIGRATION (.github#1262). It stands up the producer for the `coordination-kit`
  capability and its materialize contract. It changes NOTHING for receivers yet — the byte-copy fabric
  is untouched and still authoritative — so it is additive and safe to land on its own. The publish
  workflow, the per-receiver switch to a package reference, the `dist/dotnet` build-config half, and the
  deletion of the *-propagate / *-selftest / *-coherence family are the sequenced follow-ups ADR-0062
  names; each is its own reviewable change.

  IDENTITY IS FOREVER (ADR-0003): PackageId FS.GG.Kit is the product identity — never renamed in place.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- A content-only package: it ships kit files + a materialize target, no assembly. A TFM is still
         required for `dotnet pack`; net10.0 matches the repo's global.json floor. IncludeBuildOutput
         false means no lib/ folder is produced or packed. -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
    <GenerateDependencyFile>false</GenerateDependencyFile>

    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>true</SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking>
    <PackageId>FS.GG.Kit</PackageId>
    <!-- 0.12.0 (.github#1594): the packed `pnext-item` skill gains an ENGINE-CURRENCY step — the worker
         checks the SHARED checkout's engine against `origin/main` under the engine's own source trees
         before its first board write, and escalates when it cannot repair it. MINOR, not patch. Not
         patch because this is not the prose repair 0.8.1 (edc8404) was: it adds a REQUIRED step to the
         protocol a receiver materializes and its agents execute, so the content a consumer restores
         instructs different behaviour than 0.11.0's did. Not major because it is additive — no step is
         removed or reordered, nothing a 0.11.0 reader was told to do stops being true, and the member
         SET is unchanged (still 25 members; the change is confined to two files inside an existing
         one). Only `.claude/skills/pnext-item` moves in `registry/repos.lock`. 0.11.0 rather than
         0.10.0 is the predecessor because #1658 landed its engine-pin republish first, mid-item; the
         two were coordinated over `say` and this took the next minor rather than reusing theirs.
         PRIOR — 0.11.0 (.github#1658): republish for the fleet engine pin. The packed
         `.config/dotnet-tools.json` advances `fs.gg.coord.cli` 0.11.0 -> 0.12.0, so a receiver that
         materializes this kit restores a DIFFERENT ENGINE. MINOR, not patch: FS.GG.Coord.Cli 0.12.0 is
         itself a BREAKING minor (#1523 scoped the projections — the `json` flag now exits 1 on 20 of the
         40 verbs, the `text` flag on 4 of 40), so receiver behaviour changes and a patch would understate
         it. Not major, because the 0.x line spells breaking as a minor org-wide and the kit's own member
         SET and materialize contract are unchanged: still 25 members, same destinations, same modes.
         This is the #1615 fan-out firing exactly as that item predicts — the pin is a kit row, so an
         engine bump republishes the kit and stales every receiver. #1615 is the open decision about
         whether it should; this republish does NOT pre-empt it.
         PRIOR — 0.10.0 (.github#1586): the packed `scripts/fsgg-coord` shrinks to the resolver a receiver
         actually executes; the verb partition and the stale/dirty guards move to
         `scripts/fsgg-coord-guards.sh`, which has NO kit row and is therefore not packed at all.
         MINOR, not patch, and not major. Not major because receiver BEHAVIOUR is unchanged: the guards
         were only ever reachable from tiers 2/2b (a source build), which only the repo owning coord's
         source can have — every receiver resolves at tier 1/3/4 and reaches identical code. Not patch
         because the packed member's content changes structurally rather than in prose; e24b0bc took a
         minor (0.7.0 -> 0.8.0) for the same class (a shim verb-partition republish), while a
         comment-only shim edit took a patch (0.8.0 -> 0.8.1, edc8404). The member SET is unchanged:
         still 25 members, and the guard module is not one of them.

         0.12.0 -> 0.13.0 (.github#1673, the coord-engine 0.13.0 release). TWO packed members move, and
         either one alone would oblige this republish:
           * `dist/dotnet/.config/dotnet-tools.json` — the engine pin, 0.12.0 -> 0.13.0. A hashed kit:
             source (#1077), which is why `engine-pin-coherence` cannot be cleared without paying this.
           * `.claude/skills/pnext-item` — its `references/command-contracts.md` is GENERATED from
             `Protocol.landableExitCodes`, and .github#1667's correction to the `landable` PENDING and
             NoVerdict rows regenerated it. So the kit's own tree digest moved before the pin was touched.
         MINOR, not patch, and not major. Not major because no member is added or removed — still 25 —
         and no receiver-visible file moves destination. Not patch because both changes are substantive
         content a receiver ACTS on rather than prose: the pin changes which engine binary every receiver
         `exec`s, and the projection changes the merge recipe the fleet reads. Same class as 0.11.0 and
         0.12.0 earlier today, both of which took a minor for a pin bump.

         0.14.0 (.github#1636, ADR-0067 §5 / ADR-0065 as amended) — RETIRE `.codex/skills`.
         MINOR, and on a 0.x line that is how an observable change is spelled because there is no
         major to take (registry/dependencies.yml:835's precedent, the coord-engine release). It is
         not a patch: this is the most receiver-VISIBLE change the kit has shipped. Two things move,
         both in the build logic rather than in the member set:
           * `FsggKitSkillRoots` drops to `.claude/skills;.agents/skills`, so a receiver's next
             materialize writes TWO roots instead of three.
           * `FsggKitRetiredSkillRoots` is new, and the materialize target now SWEEPS the kit's own
             skill directories out of each retired root. Without this the retirement could never
             reach a receiver at all — the stale-file sweep only walks roots the manifest still maps
             into, so a dropped root is one the materializer stops looking at, and its copies would
             survive forever. A receiver's own skill under that root is deliberately left alone.
         The member set is UNCHANGED at 25 and no destination moves, so it is not a major.

         NOTE for the next person: `kit-published-coherence`'s PR arm was GREEN on the PR that made
         this change, because that arm keys on the six `kit:` CONTENT sources in registry/repos.yml
         and this diff touched none of them — it changed the package's own build logic instead. The
         republish obligation was real anyway. Gap filed; do not read that arm's green as "no
         republish owed". -->
    <Version>0.14.0</Version>

    <Description>FS-GG coordination kit as one versioned artifact — the shared coordination skills, the fsgg-coord client shim, and the engine tool manifest, derived from registry/repos.yml. Receivers reference it and materialize the skills onto disk (ADR-0062) instead of byte-copying the kit (ADR-0058: derive, don't restate).</Description>
    <Authors>FS-GG</Authors>
    <Company>FS-GG</Company>
    <PackageTags>fsgg;coordination;kit;skills;dotnet</PackageTags>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/FS-GG/.github</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/FS-GG/.github/tree/main/src/FS.GG.Kit</PackageProjectUrl>

    <!-- NU5128: a package with no lib/ref assembly and no dependencies. That is exactly what a
         content-only kit package IS (SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking above), not a defect. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128</NoWarn>

    <!-- Where the kit is staged before packing: under obj/ (gitignored), regenerated every pack, so
         the derived set is never committed and cannot go stale. BaseIntermediateOutputPath (obj/) is
         resolved in the SDK props imported ABOVE this body; IntermediateOutputPath is not set until the
         SDK targets run (after the body), so using it here would evaluate empty and stage into src/. -->
    <KitStagingDir>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)kit-staging</KitStagingDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Consumer-imported build logic (the materialize + verify contract) and the README, packed at the
       well-known paths NuGet auto-imports build/ from and reads the readme from. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="build/FS.GG.Kit.props"   Pack="true" PackagePath="build/" />
    <None Include="build/FS.GG.Kit.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="build/" />
    <None Include="README.md"               Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Derive the packed kit from registry/repos.yml at pack time, then add the staged files as package
       content. TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage is NuGet's supported hook for COMPUTED package
       content — items added here land in the nupkg without being static <None> globs (which MSBuild
       evaluates before StageKit could have written anything). -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>$(TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage);AddKitContent</TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="StageKit" BeforeTargets="Pack">
    <Exec Command="bash &quot;$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/stage-kit.sh&quot; &quot;$(KitStagingDir)&quot;" />
  </Target>

  <Target Name="AddKitContent" DependsOnTargets="StageKit">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_KitStaged Include="$(KitStagingDir)/**/*" />
      <!-- Constant folder PackagePath: NuGet appends each glob item's RecursiveDir-relative path
           (skills/check-board/SKILL.md, client/fsgg-coord, …) under kit/. Naming the folder rather than
           the file also sidesteps NuGet reading an extension-less destination (the fsgg-coord client)
           as a directory and doubling the path onto it. -->
      <TfmSpecificPackageFile Include="@(_KitStaged)">
        <PackagePath>kit/</PackagePath>
      </TfmSpecificPackageFile>
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
